{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And she shall draw off his shoe from upon his foot, and spit before his face.</b> Scripture requires her to draw off the shoe and the spitting before his face, that is, that she spit before him on the ground; and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: saliva visible to the eyes of the judges." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason for the commandment of levirate marriage is so that he may remain in the house of the deceased and no stranger may rule over his wife and his property, as it is written, 'the wife of the deceased shall not go outside to a strange man,' for thereby they estimate the mind of the deceased, that he would wish his brother to rule over his property and his wife rather than another, since he has no seed to inherit him — this is the reason for levirate marriage. And the reason for ḥaliṣah: as long as the levir wishes to perform levirate marriage, he is establishing seed for his brother and it is as though his brother were alive; but when he does not perform levirate marriage and does not establish seed for his brother, it appears that his brother is dead, and he is required to mourn for him. Therefore the levirate woman draws off the shoe, which is a sign of mourning, as though he were mourning for his deceased brother." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: the commandment of levirate marriage is included among the ḥuqqim, and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded — 'the saliva of the levirate woman is included among the ḥuqqim,' as it is written (Leviticus [uncertain chapter]): ״את חקותי תשמרו״ — 'You shall keep My statutes' — matters that the evil impulse and the nations of the world protest against, and these are: shaʿatnez and pork and the saliva of the levirate woman and the crossbreeding of diverse animals and the ox that is stoned and the heifer whose neck is broken and the birds of the leper and the firstling of the donkey and meat in milk and the scapegoat and the red heifer — thus they expounded in Midrash Tanḥuma in the pericah 'And these are the judgments.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the commandment of levirate marriage is of great benefit to the soul of the deceased. And know that the soul's pleasure grows greater when it transmigrates into one who is closer of the family, because it has a great affinity with him. From this our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'they dwelt brethren together' (Deuteronomy 25:5) — excluding a brother who was not in his world, for they did not have one dwelling in the world, and this one is not obligated to perform levirate marriage; and the reason is that they never recognized each other and he had no affinity with him. And the reason for ḥaliṣah, as it says, 'and she shall draw off his shoe' — when the levir does not wish to perform levirate marriage, it is indeed reckoned to him as cruelty, for he does not desire the benefit of his brother's soul, and the fitting judgment in this is that the levirate woman should draw off his shoe from upon his foot, as a sign and allusion that he is withdrawing and pulling away from the brotherhood. And this is the sense of וחלצה — from the language of (Hosea 5:6) ״חלץ מהם״; and the language of 'his shoe,' because he is locking the door before the soul of his deceased brother. It says 'from upon his foot' because the foot is the cause of walking, and so this one is a cause for his brother's soul to go to another family. And the matter of 'and she shall spit' — the loathsome spittle alludes to the loathsome thing that transmigrates from the body by way of the spinal cord with which the soul clothes and wraps itself. Therefore the masters of truth of blessed memory required that the spittle be tasteless before the levirate woman eats, so that it should not be on account of eating but rather from the moistures of her body, like that loathsome thing that comes from the moistures of the body. And the levir is cruel and loathsome in the eyes of creatures when he does not wish to perform levirate marriage, and therefore he is worthy to be called in Israel by this name, 'the house of him that has his shoe drawn off.'" "<b>And you need</b> to know that this matter is the lovingkindness of the Place toward Israel, so that all the souls may merit light in the supernal light, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'all Israel have a share in the world to come.' And this matter you will find explicitly stated in the discourse of Elihu, who said (Job 33:29–30): ״הן כל אלה יפעל אל פעמים שלש עם גבר, להשיב נפשו מני שחת לאור באור החיים״ — Scripture says: sometimes three times He illuminates from the light of life which is upon the face of the earth, in order to restore it from the pit. And it is also explicit from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself, who said to Job to confirm the words of Elihu (Job 38): ״לאחוז בכנפות הארץ וינערו רשעים ממנה, תתהפך כחומר חותם ויתיצבו כמו לבוש״ — Scripture says: 'I have commanded you like Me to take hold of the wings of the earth and to shake the wicked from it, and to set up others in their place, and the earth shall turn from them like clay of a seal which turns and changes into changing forms, and each of the wicked shall stand as a man clothed in new garments.' And after all these explanations a person can testify that this commandment, which is the commandment of levirate marriage, is founded upon this matter, and it is impossible for any objector to dispute this by way of reason, for the matter is a received tradition from the Sages, and the Sages from the prophets, back to Moses from Sinai." "<b>And in the book Bahir</b> you will find explained why the righteous suffer: because the righteous person was already wicked previously and is now being punished. But do they punish for the days of youth? And did not Rabbi Simon say that they punish only from twenty years old and upward? — I do not say this in reference to life, I say it in reference to death: that he had already been [wicked] previously. His colleagues said to him: How long will you conceal your words? He said to them: Go out and see a parable — to a man who planted a vineyard in his garden and hoped to make grapes, but it produced wild grapes. He saw that it did not succeed; he planted it again, fenced it and pruned the vines of the wild grapes and planted it a second time. He saw that it did not succeed; he fenced it and planted it after clearing it. He saw that it did not succeed; he fenced it and planted it. Until when? He said: for a thousand generations. And that is what we say: nine hundred and seventy-four generations were lacking, and the Holy One, blessed be He, stood and planted them in every single generation. Until here." "<b>Who does not build up.</b> From here is a hint that the commandment of yibum is an act of kindness from the Blessed Name. And the word יבנה is like the expression (Psalms 89): ״עולם חסד יבנה״, and this is what Qohelet said (Ecclesiastes 8:10): ״ובכן ראיתי רשעים קבורים ובאו״ — and behold, this is kindness, so that none who is banished shall be banished from Him. And thus the word בכ\"ן has the numerical value of חס\"ד."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The citation (ויקרא ט) appears to point to Leviticus, but the chapter number is uncertain; the phrase את חקותי תשמרו is Leviticus 19:19 (or 20:8). Left as printed.", 4 "The Job 38 quotations are rendered hyper-literally; the verse references and the identity of the speaker (HaShem addressing Job) follow the printed text, though the paraphrastic expansions make exact correspondence to the Masoretic difficult.", 5 "The passage appears to reproduce a discussion known from Bereshit Rabbah and parallels about the 974 generations; the phrase 'אנא לא בחיי אמרי אנא במותא אמרי' is rendered hyper-literally as the text is elliptical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:26.689755690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}